# Additional original synthesis for broad guide2; source disclosures carry exact local detail.
TOPICS=[
(41,'Swallowing and reflux disorders','Dysphagia can reflect a mechanical obstruction or impaired movement. Achalasia combines denervation with failure of lower-esophageal sphincter relaxation. Retrosternal discomfort, regurgitation and weight loss are supplied findings. Slow small meals, adapted medication delivery and selected dilation or surgery appear in the treatment overview. Hiatal hernia instead relocates part of the stomach through the diaphragm: sliding, paraesophageal and mixed types differ anatomically. Small meals and avoiding recumbency after eating are supplied measures. Detailed patient-specific selection is not supplied.','41:23-31'),
(41,'Inflammation versus a mucosal ulcer','Gastritis is inflammation of gastric mucosa. Acute examples include H. pylori, NSAIDs and chemical injury; epigastric discomfort and bleeding may occur. Removing an injurious drug and reducing acid are listed treatments. Chronic antral disease is associated with H. pylori and irritants, whereas fundal autoimmune disease targets parietal cells/intrinsic factor. Bile/alkaline reflux can cause epigastric pain and bilious vomiting. A peptic ulcer is a break through protective lining. Gastric ulcers emphasize mucosal permeability; duodenal ulcers emphasize altered defenses and acid exposure. Bleeding is a supplied stress-ulcer presentation.','41:40-52'),
(41,'Maldigestion and malabsorption comparisons','Insufficient pancreatic enzymes prevent normal nutrient breakdown; steatorrhea and weight loss are characteristic supplied effects. Lactase deficiency leaves lactose for bacterial fermentation and osmotic fluid movement, producing gas and diarrhea. Bile-salt deficiency interferes with fat processing and fat-soluble vitamins. The packet lists lipase replacement for pancreatic insufficiency, lactose restriction or lactase replacement for lactose maldigestion, and nutrition/vitamin measures for bile-salt deficiency. These are overview categories, not dosing instructions.','41:55-59'),
(41,'Inflammatory bowel disease and functional symptoms','Ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic mucosal disease in the packet. Crohn disease can affect the tract from mouth to anus with skip lesions, deep ulcers, granulomas and fistulas. Both can produce diarrhea and bleeding; those symptoms alone do not distinguish them. Medication lists include antiinflammatory and immunomodulatory categories; surgery can be required, and intestinal resection can produce short-bowel consequences. IBS differs because the supplied definition lacks a specific structural/biochemical lesion; altered sensitivity, movement, microbiota and psychosocial factors are discussed. Its constipation/diarrhea pattern directs the broad symptom approach.','41:60-70'),
(41,'Outpouchings, appendix and blood supply','Diverticulosis names asymptomatic mucosal outpouchings; diverticulitis adds inflammation, often fever and leukocytosis. The packet links dietary fiber to lower colonic pressure and lists selected bowel rest, antibiotics or surgery. Appendicitis can follow obstruction and ischemia; periumbilical/epigastric pain, tenderness and systemic symptoms can progress to perforation, peritonitis or abscess. Mesenteric vascular insufficiency can cause pain, bloody diarrhea, volume loss and shock. Its supplied therapies address perfusion, fluids, infection risk and possible surgery. Diagnostic and prevention pathways remain incomplete.','41:71-75'),
(41,'Consequences of advanced liver dysfunction','Ascites reflects abnormal fluid distribution in portal disease. The packet compares overflow, underfill and peripheral-vasodilation theories; the last combines pressure and vascular effects. Salt restriction, diuretics and selected drainage/shunting appear in the overview. Hepatic encephalopathy changes cognition, behavior and movement, including asterixis; hepatorenal syndrome adds renal failure in advanced hepatic disease. Jaundice is a sign requiring identification of its cause. Acute liver failure differs from established cirrhosis; acetaminophen overdose is the supplied leading cause and N-acetylcysteine its specified treatment. Detailed clinical management is outside the available source depth.','41:81-92'),
(41,'Different routes to hepatic injury','Autoimmune hepatitis is inflammatory immune injury; immunosuppressive therapy is listed. Alcohol-associated steatosis can reverse after cessation, while hepatitis and fibrosis represent more severe injury. NAFLD describes fat accumulation without alcohol intake. Primary biliary cholangitis involves immune injury of small intrahepatic ducts; secondary biliary cirrhosis follows common-duct obstruction. Viral hepatitis differs by route and viral dependency. These categories prevent treating all jaundice or all cirrhosis as a single mechanism.','41:93-107'),
(41,'Gallstones and pancreatic injury','Cholesterol-rich bile, stasis and related factors can promote stones. Pigmented stones have different supplied associations. A lodged stone can cause biliary colic; common-duct obstruction can cause jaundice. Cholecystitis names inflammation with fever, leukocytosis and guarding. The packet lists stone removal and cholecystectomy among selected treatments. Pancreatitis begins when cellular/duct damage lets digestive enzymes injure pancreatic tissue. Chronic disease destroys acinar and islet tissue and replaces it with fibrosis; this can require digestive enzyme and insulin support.','41:108-116'),
(41,'GI and accessory-organ tumors at supplied depth','Esophageal cancer is associated with chronic irritation/dysplasia and can produce dysphagia or chest pain. Gastric cancer begins in mucosal glands and may present late with weight loss or bleeding. Small-intestinal cancer is uncommon in the packet. Colorectal tumors can arise from polyps; right-sided and left-sided lesions differ in stool and obstructive patterns. Hepatocellular tumors arise in hepatocytes, whereas cholangiocarcinoma arises in ducts. Gallbladder cancer may be initially silent; pancreatic ductal tumors can produce pain, weight loss or jaundice. Surgery and selected oncologic therapies are listed, but staging, screening and full diagnostic pathways are absent. The disputed viral liver-cancer risk list and prognosis numbers are excluded.','41:117-129'),
(42,'Congenital obstruction and early feeding','Cleft lip/palate interfere with feeding and may need adapted feeding support and surgery. Esophageal atresia ends in a blind pouch; a tracheoesophageal fistula creates an abnormal airway-esophageal connection with aspiration risk. Pyloric muscle enlargement narrows the outlet. Intrinsic small-bowel narrowing creates dilation upstream and collapse downstream; malrotation permits bands or twisting to obstruct flow. Meckel diverticulum follows persistence of embryonic duct tissue and can cause painless rectal bleeding. Hirschsprung disease reflects failed enteric neural migration. Anorectal malformations can create a complete distal obstruction. Specific prevention and diagnostic sequences are not complete.','42:6-27'),
(42,'Pediatric nutrition and mucosal emergencies','Marasmus is deficiency of all nutrients; kwashiorkor emphasizes severe protein deficiency. Use those supported definitions rather than the inconsistent slide60 labels. Faltering growth has biologic, psychosocial and environmental causes; the supplied approach addresses both underlying disease and feeding context. NEC combines immature barrier and ischemic/inflammatory injury and may perforate the bowel. The packet lists stopping feeds, decompression, fluids/electrolytes, antibiotics and selected surgery. These are source-depth concepts, not a neonatal treatment protocol.','42:43-54'),
(42,'Newborn bilirubin and bile drainage','The packet distinguishes benign transient first-week jaundice from pathologic concerns such as appearance within the first day. Toxic unconjugated bilirubin can enter the brain and produce kernicterus. Phototherapy and selected exchange transfusion are named interventions, but the slide wavelength wording is excluded. Biliary atresia obstructs extrahepatic drainage and causes jaundice; surgical drainage and liver transplantation are listed. Numeric thresholds and treatment selection require the missing textbook or instructor clarification.','42:61-64'),
(43,'Bone arrangement and source-depth measurements','Compact bone uses Haversian organization; spongy bone uses trabeculae and contains red marrow spaces. Periosteum carries vessels and nerves, while endosteum lines marrow cavities. Long bones have diaphysis, metaphysis and epiphysis; flat and short bones arrange compact and spongy layers differently. The packet lists imaging, serum calcium/phosphorus, bone-specific alkaline phosphatase and telopeptides for skeletal assessment. Joint assessment can include direct arthroscopy and fluid analysis; muscular assessment includes CK, urine myoglobin, EMG and biopsy. These tests do not fill the missing muscle-mass laboratory association.','43:22-26,61-65'),
(44,'Tumor tissue is different from tumor location','Osteogenic tumors form bone or osteoid; chondrogenic tumors form cartilage. Osteosarcoma is emphasized in adolescents/young adults and long-bone metaphyses, often near the knee. Chondrosarcoma is described in older adults and produces chondroid. Fibrosarcoma produces fibrous tissue and may cause pathologic fracture; myelogenic tumors arise from marrow-related cells. Destructive patterns can be geographic, moth-eaten or permeative. These are supplied recognition categories, not a complete oncologic workup.','44:42-47'),
(45,'Spinal, foot and connective-tissue distinctions','Metatarsus adductus changes the forefoot; equinovarus turns the heel inward and plantar-flexes the foot; pes planus describes a flat arch. Scoliosis is a rotational curvature when structural. OI is a collagen disorder with fractures and possible extraosseous findings. Juvenile arthritis emphasizes large joints and uveitis in the supplied comparison. These examples do not establish the complete pediatric connective-tissue disease inventory requested by guide41.','45:20-28,34-36')]
